//? Title: Convolutional Autoencoders: Reconstructing from Spatial Codes
//? Subtitle: Exact pooling adjoints, an executed 741-parameter image model, and a visible MNIST fit with spatial-code controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Reconstructing an image from a smaller representation requires retaining information about both
appearance and location. A convolutional autoencoder learns local filters before reducing spatial
resolution, then learns a decoder that expands the resulting feature maps. This report checks a
four-by-four pooling example and its complete input gradient, derives the convolution and repetition
adjoints, and trains the original 741-parameter model on genuine MNIST. Its spatial bottleneck is
7 by 7 by 4. Validation selects a checkpoint; separate held-out images compare the reconstruction
with untrained, fitting-mean, pooling, zero-code and shuffled-code controls. The experiment concerns
pixel reconstruction: held-out MSE is 0.01778 versus 0.06780 for a fitting-mean image.
It makes no classification, denoising or compressed-file claim.
)
\toc

> The method

A grayscale image \(X\in[0,1]^{H\times W\times1}\), with dimensions divisible by four, becomes
an image-shaped code \(Z\in\mathbb R^{H/4\times W/4\times4}\). Four independent learned convolutions
and fixed spatial operations define the complete reconstruction path. Let \(C_{K,b}\) mean a
stride-one, same-padded 3 by 3 cross-correlation plus bias, \(A_2\) a nonoverlapping 2 by 2 average,
\(U_2\) nearest repetition, \(\rho\) ReLU, and \(\sigma\) the sigmoid. Then

$$
Z=A_2\rho\!\left(C_{K_2,b_2}\left(A_2\rho(C_{K_1,b_1}(X))\right)\right),\qquad
\hat X=\sigma\!\left(C_{K_4,b_4}\left(U_2\rho(C_{K_3,b_3}(U_2 Z))\right)\right).
$$
\alttext(Two ReLU convolutions each followed by average pooling encode the image. Nearest repetition, a ReLU convolution, another repetition and a sigmoid convolution reconstruct it.)

There is no skip or dense bypass. The code has one quarter as many real coordinates as the input:
\((H/4)(W/4)4=HW/4\). Four channels preserve more information than a single pooled intensity map,
but they are not a guaranteed lossless representation. Quantization, entropy coding, model storage
and a shared decoder would be needed to specify a compression bit rate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/convolutional_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(58, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image\n28 × 28 × 1")
      .node("e", {5, 0}, "Learn local filters\nReLU + mean pool, twice")
      .node("z", {10, 0}, "Spatial code\n7 × 7 × 4")
      .node("d", {10, -3}, "Repeat + convolution\nTwice, sigmoid output")
      .node("r", {5, -3}, "Reconstruction\n28 × 28 × 1")
      .node("l", {0, -3}, "Pixel MSE\nAdam updates all four filters")
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "z")
      .edge("z", "d")
      .edge("d", "r")
      .edge("r", "l")
      .edge("x", "l", "Target")
      .save_svg("build/convolutional_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/convolutional_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The code is spatial: neighbouring code cells describe neighbouring input regions. Only the input image supplies the target.)
\alttext(A diagram follows an image through learned local features and a smaller spatial code, then expansion and reconstruction, with the input and reconstruction meeting at pixel MSE.)

For a batch of \(N\) images, mean squared error averages every pixel,
\(L=\sum(\hat X-X)^2/(NHW)\). Its output-logit derivative is
\(\Delta=2(\hat X-X)\hat X(1-\hat X)/(NHW)\). A convolution's shared kernel receives contributions
from every image and location. For input \(V\), offset \((a,b)\), input channel \(c\), output channel
\(o\), and preactivation adjoint \(G\), the local chain rule gives

$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial K_{abco}}=\sum_{n,y,x} V_{n,y+a-1,x+b-1,c}G_{nyxo},\qquad
\frac{\partial L}{\partial b_o}=\sum_{n,y,x}G_{nyxo}.
$$
\alttext(A kernel gradient sums input times output adjoint over all images and output positions. A bias gradient sums the output adjoint. Input values outside the image are zero.)

Each input adjoint accumulates \(K_{abco}G_{nyxo}\) over the output positions that used it.
ReLU passes this adjoint where its preactivation is positive; at zero this implementation uses
zero. The two fixed spatial operators have different adjoints:

$$
(A_2V)_{ijc}=\tfrac14\sum_{a,b=0}^1 V_{2i+a,2j+b,c},\qquad
(U_2Z)_{2i+a,2j+b,c}=Z_{ijc},
$$
\alttext(Average pooling averages a two by two block. Nearest repetition copies one code value into each of four positions.)

Thus \(A_2^*G=U_2G/4\) and \(U_2^*G=4A_2G\). Repeating values does not recover details discarded by
pooling: it merely provides a larger grid for a learned convolution. It also differs from tiling
the whole image and from a transposed convolution. Both encoder and decoder keep ordinary,
independent convolution kernels in this example.

> A hand-checkable four-by-four image

Use row-major values \(X_{yx}=(4y+x)/15\). Its 2 by 2 pool is
\(((1/6,3/10),(7/10,5/6))\). Repeating these four means produces a blockwise-constant image.
Within every original block the residuals sum to zero; consequently, for
\(L=\|U_2A_2X-X\|^2/16\), the indirect derivative through the mean vanishes and
\(\partial L/\partial X_{yx}=(X_{yx}-(U_2A_2X)_{yx})/8\).
The MSE is exactly \(17/900\). All 16 predictions and all 16 derivatives are checked below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/convolutional_toy.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n              = dm::nn;
  const auto          type = n::DType::Float64;
  std::vector<double> pixels(16);
  for (int i = 0; i < 16; ++i)
    pixels[i] = double(i) / 15;
  const auto x          = n::from_vector(pixels, {1, 4, 4, 1}).requires_grad_();
  const auto code       = n::avg_pool2d(x, 2, 2);
  const auto prediction = n::upsample_nearest(code, 2);
  const auto loss       = n::mse(prediction, x);
  const auto gradient   = n::grad(loss, {x})[0];
  double     worst      = 0;
  for (int y = 0; y < 4; ++y) {
    for (int col = 0; col < 4; ++col) {
      const double mean = (4 * (2 * (y / 2)) + 2 * (col / 2) + 2.5) / 15;
      if (std::abs(prediction.at({0, y, col, 0}) - mean) > 1e-14)
        return 1;
      worst =
          std::max(worst, std::abs(gradient.at({0, y, col, 0}) - (pixels[4 * y + col] - mean) / 8));
    }
  }
  const n::TensorFunction f = [](const auto& a) {
    return n::mse(n::upsample_nearest(n::avg_pool2d(a[0], 2, 2), 2), a[0]);
  };
  if (worst > 1e-14 || std::abs(loss.item() - 17. / 900) > 1e-14 || !n::gradcheck(f, {x}).ok)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "Pool: {}.\n\nMSE {:.12f}; all 16 gradient errors <= {:.2e}; finite differences pass.\n",
      code.reshape({2, 2}).values_str(),
      loss.item(),
      worst);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat({x, prediction, n::abs(prediction - x)}, 0),
      "build/convolutional_toy.png",
      3,
      0,
      1,
      2,
      .8,
      48);
}
```
