\caption(Columns are digits 0 through 9; rows are the eight predictors described above. Integer enlargement is three, with two-pixel gaps. Pooling produces visible blocks; the learned decoder can smooth and refine spatial structure.)
\alttext(An eight-row reconstruction contact sheet compares original held-out digits with learned outputs, errors and five baseline controls.)

The optional development check `scripts/check_convolutional_autoencoder.py` reads these native
archives without preparing data or fitting. An independent Float64 NumPy forward/adjoint computation
checks all 741 first-batch parameter derivatives (maximum absolute difference below 1e-8), all
156,800 held-out predictions (below 7.1e-7) and all six retained activation maps. A separate Float32
calculation verifies the first Adam update bit for bit. All six parameter snapshots also match a
run of the original C++ program bit for bit. These numerical checks complement the visibly retained
fit; they do not substitute another framework's training trajectory.

> Conclusion

Shared convolution kernels let a small model learn local reconstruction rules with a spatial code.
The toy example makes the exact loss of within-block information and the pooling adjoint visible;
the complete program exposes every Adam update and keeps checkpoint selection separate from test
evaluation. Pixel MSE and a small gallery describe intensity agreement, not semantic representation,
a denoising guarantee, a generative prior or a general-purpose compression format. Three epochs on
1,000 fitting images are a reproducible small experiment, not a full-MNIST benchmark. This untied,
average-pooling and nearest-repeat variant also differs from the architecture and stacked
pretraining experiments of Masci and colleagues.

> References

- Masci, J., Meier, U., Cireşan, D. and Schmidhuber, J. (2011). Stacked Convolutional Auto-Encoders for Hierarchical Feature Extraction.
  [Author-hosted ICANN paper](https://people.idsia.ch/~ciresan/data/icann2011.pdf).
- Goodfellow, I., Bengio, Y. and Courville, A. (2016). *Deep Learning*,
  [convolutional networks](https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/convnets.html) and
  [autoencoders](https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/autoencoders.html).
- LeCun, Y., Bottou, L., Bengio, Y. and Haffner, P. (1998). Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition.
  [Proceedings of the IEEE 86(11), 2278–2324](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.726791).
